WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.663
A conditional mutation joins the decision

2
00:00:02.663 --> 00:00:05.367
and state transition at the authoritative
store.

3
00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:07.891
The inventory statement decrements
quantity only for

4
00:00:07.891 --> 00:00:10.595
the requested record while quantity is
positive.

5
00:00:10.595 --> 00:00:13.930
Under the database's documented atomic
update semantics, competing writers

6
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.355
cannot both rely on an obsolete
application-side availability check.

7
00:00:17.355 --> 00:00:20.329
One writer succeeds; another may find no
qualifying

8
00:00:20.329 --> 00:00:23.754
row or encounter a concurrency error that
needs handling.

9
00:00:23.754 --> 00:00:25.917
The TypeScript repository must implement
that

10
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:27.900
condition inside the mutation itself.

11
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.784
A method named decrementIfAvailable
provides no guarantee if

12
00:00:30.784 --> 00:00:33.849
its implementation first reads and then
independently writes.

13
00:00:33.849 --> 00:00:36.012
Inspect the generated query or storage

14
00:00:36.012 --> 00:00:38.445
API, including transaction and consistency
settings.

15
00:00:38.445 --> 00:00:40.248
Parameterize inputs and validate
quantities

16
00:00:40.248 --> 00:00:42.231
before they reach the operation.

17
00:00:42.231 --> 00:00:43.493
Check the result.

18
00:00:43.493 --> 00:00:47.188
For a unique item identifier, exactly one
changed row establishes

19
00:00:47.188 --> 00:00:50.703
that this transition occurred, subject to
successful transaction commit.

20
00:00:50.703 --> 00:00:54.038
Zero means no row satisfied the predicate;
the item

21
00:00:54.038 --> 00:00:57.643
might be unavailable, missing, or outside
the permitted scope.

22
00:00:57.643 --> 00:00:59.626
Do not report success anyway.

23
00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:02.510
Map the outcome to the application's
intended error

24
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.025
policy, and distinguish it from
infrastructure or serialization failures.

25
00:01:06.025 --> 00:01:09.631
The same approach supports moving an
approval from pending to

26
00:01:09.631 --> 00:01:13.146
approved, claiming an eligible job,
enforcing a quota, or

27
00:01:13.146 --> 00:01:16.931
applying a debit only when a balance
condition still holds.

28
00:01:16.931 --> 00:01:19.996
The mutation must include the full
relevant precondition.

29
00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:22.519
Updating a counter atomically does not
automatically

30
00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:25.223
protect a rule involving a different
record.

31
00:01:25.223 --> 00:01:26.846
Constraints add authoritative guards.

32
00:01:26.846 --> 00:01:30.181
A unique constraint can prevent duplicate
redemption records, a

33
00:01:30.181 --> 00:01:33.515
foreign key can enforce a required
relationship, and a

34
00:01:33.515 --> 00:01:36.940
supported check constraint can reject an
invalid stored quantity.

35
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.365
Choose appropriate nullability and
constraint semantics for the database.

36
00:01:40.365 --> 00:01:42.529
Application validation remains valuable
for clear

37
00:01:42.529 --> 00:01:45.233
feedback and rules the store cannot
express.

38
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:47.396
Storage enforcement protects against
competing writers

39
00:01:47.396 --> 00:01:49.379
that bypass an earlier check.

40
00:01:49.379 --> 00:01:53.074
Authorization remains separate: a
perfectly atomic transition can still be

41
00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:57.220
performed by someone who should not be
allowed to make it.

